# Append-only team records. Every branch adds an entry at the end (or, for
# STATUS.md, at the top), so two branches touching them conflict every single
# time even though neither edits the other's text. That happened three times in
# one afternoon once builders started running in parallel, and a conflict you
# resolve by keeping both sides is a conflict a merge driver should resolve.
#
# `union` keeps both sides rather than failing. It is right here precisely
# BECAUSE these files are append-only: nobody rewrites an existing entry, so
# there is no case where one side should win.
#
# It is NOT right for anything with syntax — a union merge of source or JSON
# produces a file that parses as nonsense and looks merged. Do not extend this
# list to code.
team/DECISION-LOG.md merge=union
team/STATUS.md       merge=union

# Ordering caveat, so it is not a surprise: union appends both sides in an
# order git chooses, so a STATUS.md entry may land below a newer one. Read the
# dates, and reorder in a follow-up commit if it matters.

# Generated artefacts: check out with LF, exactly as their generator writes
# them.
#
# core.autocrlf is true on the Windows dev machines, so git was handing these
# files back with CRLF while `pnpm ui:tokens` and `pnpm ui:fetch-fonts` write
# LF. The drift check ("regenerating changes nothing") therefore FAILED on the
# first run in a fresh checkout and passed on the second, because run one
# rewrote every line ending. Found by the T-0002 builder while investigating a
# failure that had nothing to do with its change — which is the cost of a flake
# nobody has chased: the next person spends their time on it instead.
#
# Diagnosed once before, in CI, as "prettier reformatted the generated file".
# Same root cause, and it was misattributed then.
packages/ui/src/tokens.colours.generated.ts  text eol=lf
packages/ui/src/tokens.generated.css         text eol=lf
packages/ui/src/fonts.generated.css          text eol=lf
packages/ui/tailwind-preset.cjs              text eol=lf
